# NIST CSF Incident Analysis

## Goal

The goal of this project was to analyze a simulated cybersecurity incident using the National Institute of Standards and Technology Cybersecurity Framework (NIST CSF).

The scenario involved a distributed denial-of-service (DDoS) attack that disrupted a fictional organization's network through a flood of ICMP traffic.

Using information gathered during the incident investigation, I applied the five NIST CSF functions—Identify, Protect, Detect, Respond, and Recover—to evaluate 
the incident and develop a strategy for improving the organization's network security.

This project was completed as part of the Google Cybersecurity Professional Certificate.

## Skills Practiced

- NIST Cybersecurity Framework
- Incident analysis
- Incident response planning
- Network security
- DDoS attack analysis
- Firewall security
- Network monitoring
- IDS/IPS concepts
- SIEM concepts
- Security documentation

## What I Learned

This project helped me understand how a cybersecurity framework can provide structure when analyzing and responding to a security incident.

Rather than treating an incident as a single technical problem, the NIST CSF encouraged me to consider multiple stages of security:

- Identifying vulnerabilities and affected systems
- Protecting assets by improving security controls
- Detecting suspicious activity through monitoring
- Responding to and containing incidents
- Recovering systems and restoring normal operations

The exercise also demonstrated how multiple security controls can work together. Firewall configuration, network monitoring, IDS/IPS, and centralized alert monitoring can 
each contribute to detecting or limiting malicious network activity.

